{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2110 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4e62b7b716f05510b51fc0b5b37693d7c2f22aed5cd282d33b60fd18c29621df" :edb/db-before-t 2109 :edb/db-after-t 2110 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535422 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:47:35.568Z" 13194139535422 true] [17592186087681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348445 13194139535422 true] [17592186087682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348451 13194139535422 true] [17592186087682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348461 13194139535422 true] [17592186087746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348454 13194139535422 true] [17592186088392 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535422 true] [17592186088392 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/90b505954bb714ad86f60e07f7a26c16c954139689d96684ed553a7b494f0527.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186088392 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/q-89345.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sqrt{2}\\sec t, y = 2\\tan t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139535422 false] [17592186088392 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/b43dcf6c343994e3226e2840a0ae80f60484c8de5d7984f61ec6447185181a76.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348446 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1089 "q-325917" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/a1506b2272710ce21e0c58d327738dbe4afb3b134981a239e6e7882c3efb7072.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1113 #uuid "efde2ef3-da00-5f72-b88d-7d7aef4b94cc" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348445 1145 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(4,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=4.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348447 13194139535422 true] [17592186348446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348448 13194139535422 true] [17592186348446 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348446 1041 "field-1" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348446 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348448 13194139535422 true] [17592186348446 1110 #uuid "7037f9c0-08ef-5855-ae15-a2d1779c3832" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348447 1034 "3\\sec(t)" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348447 1111 #uuid "f429916c-f2e5-545e-baf3-3e4f2b8f4a33" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348448 1034 "4\\sec(t)" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348448 1111 #uuid "f890c957-ce64-57a6-b8dc-9900706f03dd" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348450 13194139535422 true] [17592186348449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348449 1041 "field-2" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348450 13194139535422 true] [17592186348449 1110 #uuid "a25aa1f4-f9e1-553e-88f3-64ad734a5b87" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348450 1034 "6" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348450 1111 #uuid "1998fd88-1bb1-54f2-8d00-bb827ce3ebc0" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348451 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348452 13194139535422 true] [17592186348451 1089 "q-325940" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535422 true] [17592186348451 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348451 1113 #uuid "8c078f87-d5f5-5fde-be7c-c314f1607cbe" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348451 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348452 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348452 1041 "field-1" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348452 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348452 1110 #uuid "f1181f40-9254-5b17-b74f-931115abd8cc" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348453 1034 "\\frac{x^2}{81}-\\frac{y^2}{16}" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348453 1111 #uuid "2170bc3f-f3e3-5eab-8d33-26d523add882" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348455 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348457 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348459 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1089 "q-325981" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/7facf793b5ce6740a31d665d96b03dd591e7edc2a6c35e519fa3b805534e02c1.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$\n\n$t\\in [$\n$-\\pi ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1113 #uuid "6212fbd3-023a-506d-a740-9d9958318e9e" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348454 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/80e49fe1187d1b9e42ef6e221568c9bafca1507238cc644afbda695f5cee2539.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\text{ }\\cup \\text{ }(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348456 13194139535422 true] [17592186348455 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348455 1041 "field-1" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348455 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348456 13194139535422 true] [17592186348455 1110 #uuid "4b4eb23f-7d8c-5a4f-a59e-5792b02417a5" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348456 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348456 1111 #uuid "3d88f23e-d694-5796-8013-4a6b1b9b14eb" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348457 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348458 13194139535422 true] [17592186348457 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348457 1041 "field-2" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348457 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348458 13194139535422 true] [17592186348457 1110 #uuid "6b7fb731-9191-519f-8736-5b7d21b6db36" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348458 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348458 1111 #uuid "45c381d6-bc84-55d4-8f58-bec3fc4c32be" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348460 13194139535422 true] [17592186348459 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535422 true] [17592186348459 1041 "field-3" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348459 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348460 13194139535422 true] [17592186348459 1110 #uuid "a739d859-bc53-59a2-8408-3a94e51820c8" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348460 1034 "\\pi" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348460 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535422 true] [17592186348460 1111 #uuid "e5136f1b-23f4-5715-8858-5f369f611646" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348461 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348462 13194139535422 true] [17592186348461 1089 "q-7988" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348461 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535422 true] [17592186348461 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348461 1113 #uuid "5237c29a-53d8-5c9a-b323-596badbbe2da" 13194139535422 true] [17592186348461 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get]\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 1